<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Trigly mark as a status-bar icon. Not the launcher icon: Android draws a
  notification's small icon as a silhouette, tinted by the system, so only the
  alpha channel survives and the colours in `ic_launcher_foreground.xml` would
  be thrown away — while its generous adaptive-icon margins would remain, and
  the glyph would come out too small to read at 24dp.

  The tint the system applies is the app's own: `EngineService` sets it with
  `NotificationCompat.Builder.setColor`, and the platform paints this
  silhouette in that colour after holding it to 4.5:1 against the shade. So
  this file is already the answer to "the mark with no background colour", and
  it is the surface where the chosen scheme does come through. See
  `EngineService.notificationColor`. The white below is only what renders
  before any tint is applied.

  Same three shapes and the same numbers as `docs/branding/trigly-mark.svg`, on
  the same 108 board so they can be compared line by line. Only the scale
  differs.

  It is 1.5, and it used to be 1.98. The old number came from the maximum a
  status-bar icon is allowed to fill: the mark spans y 29..79, so 50 of 108
  units, and a 22dp content area inside a 24dp canvas is 99 of 108, hence
  99/50. Filling the whole allowance turned out to be the wrong reading of it.
  In a notification the mark then touched the top and bottom of its slot and
  sat visibly heavier than every other app's icon in the same shade, because
  those leave a margin rather than claim the maximum. 1.5 puts the mark at 75
  of 108 units, a little over two thirds of the box, which is where the rest of
  the shade sits.

  The cost is at the other end, in the status bar, where the icon is a few
  pixels tall and smaller artwork is harder to pick out. That is the trade, and
  it was made looking at a real shade on a real phone.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108"
    android:tint="#FFFFFFFF">

    <group
        android:pivotX="54"
        android:pivotY="54"
        android:scaleX="1.5"
        android:scaleY="1.5">

        <!-- The bar of the T. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M32,29 h44 v12 h-44 z" />

        <!-- The stem, running into the arrowhead rather than stopping short. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M49,41 h10 v22 h-10 z" />

        <!-- The arrowhead. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
            android:pathData="M42,63 h24 l-12,16 z" />
    </group>
</vector>
